in this lesson we're going to talk about  an important concept for the new sat and  that's the right triangle trigonometry  or oftentimes referred to as the  sohcahtoa  trig ratios so let's go through what  sohcahtoa represents and then we'll get  into some example problems to give you  some practice so first of all this  acronym kind of helps us to remember the  sine cosine and the tangent ratios and  what it is it's always going to be the  second letter divided by the third so in  this case it's the opposite over the  hypotenuse  when you're finding the sine  the cosine is the adjacent side over the  hypotenuse when you're finding the  cosine and the tangent is going to be  the opposite side divided by the  adjacent side so let's look at an  example so say you have a 3 four five  right triangle like this and you want to  find the sine of theta well sine is  opposite over hypotenuse so opposite is  going to be across that's three the  hypotenuse is across from the right  angle that's five so the sine is going  to be three-fifths if you want to find  the cosine cosine is adjacent which  means next to okay but not the  hypotenuse so the other side that's next  to that's four  over the hypotenuse that's going to be  five and then the tangent is going to be  the opposite side  over the adjacent side it's the opposite  of adjacent so that's going to be three  over four so those are your basic you  know trig ratios sine cosine and tangent  now you can also find the cosecant  secant and cotangent these are called  the reciprocal identities the cosecant  is really just the reciprocal of sine so  if you know the sine is three-fifths the  cosecant would be five-thirds  secant is one over cosine or the  reciprocal of cosine and cotangent is a  reciprocal of tangent now another  problem that kind of comes up on the sat  is this concept of the 90 minus the  angle 90 minus theta  and the idea here is that see if this  angle is 90 these two angles have to add  up to 90. so if this is theta this angle  over here is 90 minus theta so for  example like if this is 20 this has to  be 90 minus 20 or 70 because 70 plus 20  is 90 plus 90 is 180 all the angles in a  triangle add up to 180 right  so let's just say they ask us to find  the sine of 90 minus theta what we can  do is we can position ourselves at this  angle and think about the opposite over  hypotenuse so opposite is going to be 4  in this case and the hypotenuse is going  to be 5 so this is 4 fifths so if you  can just remember that these angles are  complementary or they add up to 90  degrees if this is theta this is 90  minus theta and you got it so let's go  through some examples  the first example says in a right  triangle one angle measures x degrees  where the tangent of x equals 5 12  what is the cosine of 90 minus x so go  ahead and pause the video see if you can  do this on your own and we'll go through  it together  so what i would do is i would draw a  right triangle like this  here's angle x  and this of course would then be 90  minus x  and it tells us that the tangent of this  angle  which is opposite  over adjacent  observer adjacent so that's a cross and  next to right and if we do the  pythagorean theorem a squared plus b  squared equals c squared or if we know  our pythagorean triples we can find the  hypotenuse here and that's going to be  13. so all i did was i did 5 squared  plus 12 squared equals c squared that's  25 plus 144 equals c squared 169 equals  c squared and if you take the square  root of both sides  you'll get 13 for that hypotenuse but  then it says you know what's the cosine  of 90 minus x that's this angle here so  we're positioning ourselves at the 90  minus x angle  cosine of course is adjacent over  hypotenuse so adjacent is next to  hypotenuse is 13. so the answer to this  question is going to be 5 13.  okay let's go to example number two so  here they give us this triangle with  angles m and n okay and it's a right  triangle and it says if the tangent of  angle m  is 0.7  what is the cotangent  of angle n okay now there's a couple  different ways you can do this problem  but let's just look so tangent  is  opposite over adjacent now 0.7 you could  write this as 7 10 right so the opposite  okay would be 7 the adjacent would be 10  and then if you do the pythagorean  theorem you can find this side here  across from the right angle the  hypotenuse the c side  but then it says what's the cotangent of  n so we're over here at angle n  okay and we know that cotangent is the  reciprocal of tangent so tangent is  going to actually be opposite over  adjacent that's ten sevenths  but cotangent is a reciprocal of tangent  so this is going to be seven tenths so  that's probably the way that i would  approach this problem just you know go  ahead and find the the sides position  yourself at the angle that you're  interested in and then you can figure  out what the ratio is from there so  again something definitely important to  understand for the sat that's right  triangle trig and the sohcahtoa acronym  so review this lesson if you need to  otherwise i'll see you in the next  lesson
 

